        <body>
            <div xml:id="d254">

                <pb facs="0474" n="188" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg188"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 289</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 25, 1914</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to Mr. Barclay’s notes Nos. 252 and 259 of the 4th
                    and 9th of August, respectively, fully explaining the position taken up by His
                    Majesty’s Government in regard to the question of armed merchantmen, I have the
                    honour, in view of the fact that a number of British armed merchantmen will now
                    be visiting United States ports, to reiterate that the arming of British
                    merchantmen is solely a precautionary measure adopted for the purpose of defence
                    against attack from hostile craft.</p>
                <p>I have at the same time been instructed by His Majesty’s Principal Secretary of
                    State for Foreign Affairs to give the United States Government the fullest
                    assurances that British merchant vessels will never be used for purposes of
                    attack, that they are merely peaceful traders armed only for defence, that they
                    will never fire unless first fired upon, and that they will never under any
                    circumstances attack any vessel.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cecil Spring Rice</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>











